input.summary:
Current (2026) sources largely validate the repo's Option B architecture: Pipecat is a real, actively-used orchestration framework that natively wires OpenAI-compatible endpoints (proprietary APIs, Ollama, vLLM) into cascaded STT->LLM->TTS pipelines, ships Piper as a built-in TTS integration, and has dedicated OpenAI service classes plus a full speech-to-speech alternative (OpenAIRealtimeLLMService). Deepgram Flux gives Pipecat production-grade barge-in handling via acoustic+semantic turn detection, and a documented Cerebrium example proves the exact swap pattern the spec wants (Llama 3.1 8B via vLLM behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint). The one substantive update to the spec: Kyutai Unmute, named as the "fully open" future migration target, is real and does support swappable OpenAI-compatible LLM backends, but it is not a lightweight drop-in — its default local deployment needs 16GB+ GPU VRAM, Kyutai's own production instance actually runs a 120B model via OpenRouter rather than the small local default, and its LLM->TTS bridge chunks on word boundaries (not sentence boundaries), so "fully open, low-infra" is only half-true and any sentence-boundary-streaming assumption should be revised. Separately, Pipecat has no native LiteLLM integration (a year-old community request never merged) but doesn't need one, since 20+ providers including Ollama are already built in via the same OpenAI-compatible abstraction — LiteLLM is a viable but unnecessary extra layer, useful mainly for its `hosted_vllm`/`llamafile` prefixes if going that route.

Executive verdict on Option B: architecture is sound and current; recommend flagging (1) Unmute's real VRAM/infra floor, (2) Unmute's word- not sentence-level TTS chunking, and (3) that native Pipecat provider support makes LiteLLM optional rather than required, in the spec doc.</summary>
<findings>
[
  {
    "claim": "Pipecat is a real, actively-maintained open-source Python framework that orchestrates STT->LLM->TTS pipelines with built-in audio transport, turn-taking, and interruption handling, and natively supports 20+ LLM providers (including Ollama, Anthropic, OpenAI) and 20+ TTS providers (including Piper) through a shared, swappable service abstraction -- directly validating the spec's core orchestration choice and 'swappable OpenAI-compatible endpoint' design.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/pipecat-integration", "https://docs.cerebrium.ai/v4/examples/realtime-voice-agents", "https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat"],
    "evidence": "3-0 unanimous verification on all three underlying claims via primary sources (Pipecat README/docs, Deepgram integration docs, Cerebrium docs). Ollama support confirmed to work by extending OpenAILLMService and defaulting to Ollama's own OpenAI-compatible endpoint -- an even tighter match to the spec's design than a generic claim would suggest."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Pipecat provides distinct, documented building blocks for wiring an existing LLM into a real-time voice pipeline: OpenAISTTService/OpenAIRealtimeSTTService (transcription), OpenAILLMService (chat-completions reasoning), OpenAITTSService (speech synthesis), and an alternative fully speech-to-speech path via OpenAIRealtimeLLMService using OpenAI's newest realtime models (gpt-realtime-2, gpt-realtime-1.5, gpt-realtime).",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://docs.pipecat.ai/guides/features/openai-audio-models-and-apis"],
    "evidence": "3-0 unanimous, verified verbatim against Pipecat's own official docs plus API reference and GitHub source; independently confirmed gpt-realtime-1.5 is a real Feb-2026 OpenAI model, ruling out fabrication."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Deepgram's Flux STT model, integrated into Pipecat, provides built-in turn detection using combined acoustic and semantic cues, giving a concrete, production-ready mechanism for barge-in/interruption handling (a named gotcha for first-time builders).",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://developers.deepgram.com/docs/pipecat-integration"],
    "evidence": "3-0 unanimous; primary vendor docs plus Pipecat's own API reference show dedicated turn-detection event handlers (on_start_of_turn, on_end_of_turn, on_eager_end_of_turn) and Flux auto-configuring turn strategies with no manual setup needed."
  },
  {
    "claim": "A concrete, working precedent exists for the exact 'swap-friendly, self-hosted open-weight model' pattern the spec wants: a documented example pipeline runs Llama 3.1 8B via the vLLM framework, exposed as an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, inside a Pipecat voice pipeline explicitly designed so LLM/TTS can be swapped.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://docs.cerebrium.ai/v4/examples/realtime-voice-agents"],
    "evidence": "3-0 unanimous, verified against primary docs, mirrored GitHub example, and independent third-party writeup. Note: the reported ~500ms round-trip latency figure attributed to this pipeline was separately checked and REFUTED (0-3) -- do not cite that latency number as validated."
  },
  {
    "claim": "LiteLLM can serve as a universal OpenAI-compatible routing layer (prefix a model name with 'openai/' to hit any generic OpenAI-compatible endpoint, or use provider-specific prefixes like hosted_vllm/llamafile for self-hosted servers to avoid faking an API key) -- but Pipecat itself has no native/merged LiteLLM integration despite a standing community request (issue #1432, duplicate of #1352, opened 2025), because Pipecat's own 20+ built-in provider services (including Ollama/vLLM-compatible endpoints) already deliver the same swap-friendly OpenAI-compatible abstraction without needing LiteLLM as an extra layer.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/providers/openai_compatible", "https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat/issues/1432"],
    "evidence": "Mechanism claims [13] (2-1) and [14] (3-0) confirmed against LiteLLM's own docs. Issue-history claim [12] (3-0) confirmed against the actual GitHub issue. Note: stronger narrative claims about *why* Pipecat never merged LiteLLM (maintainer intent, deployer testimonials, 'declining to build it' framing) were separately checked and REFUTED (0-3 each) -- only the bare historical fact (issue opened, duplicate-closed, LiteLLM's routing mechanism) is verified, not any interpretation of maintainer motive."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Kyutai Unmute (the spec's named future 'fully open' migration target) genuinely supports a swappable, OpenAI-compatible LLM backend (VLLM self-hosted, Ollama local, or OpenAI/OpenRouter commercial API, chosen purely via env-var config) -- but is NOT a lightweight, low-infra drop-in: its default local deployment requires a minimum of 16GB GPU VRAM even for the smallest bundled model (Gemma 3 1B), and Kyutai's own production/hosted instance of Unmute actually runs a much larger model (GPT OSS 120B) via OpenRouter rather than the small local default -- undercutting any assumption that 'fully open' means minimal self-hosting burden.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://mintlify.wiki/kyutai-labs/unmute/architecture/llm-integration", "https://github.com/kyutai-labs/unmute"],
    "evidence": "Both claims 3-0 unanimous, verified directly against the official kyutai-labs/unmute GitHub README (16GB VRAM minimum, Gemma 3 1B default, GPT OSS 120B in production; CUDA/x86_64 only, no CPU or native macOS/Windows path documented)."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Unmute's LLM output is rechunked to WORD boundaries (not sentence boundaries) before being handed to its TTS engine -- a concrete technical detail (via a `rechunk_to_words` function) that should update any assumption in the spec that sentence-boundary streaming is the standard or only low-latency LLM->TTS handoff mechanism.",
    "confidence": "high",
    "sources": ["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kyutai-labs/unmute/main/unmute/llm/llm_utils.py", "https://github.com/kyutai-labs/unmute/blob/main/unmute/unmute_handler.py"],
    "evidence": "3-0 unanimous on the underlying fact, confirmed directly in Unmute's actual source code (docstring explicitly explains word-level chunking prevents TTS mispronouncing split words). CITATION CORRECTION: the originally-cited URL (mintlify.wiki/kyutai-labs/unmute/...) is a third-party AI-generated documentation mirror, not an official/primary Kyutai source -- cite the actual GitHub source files above instead. Separately, a related claim that Unmute streams via Server-Sent Events with text deltas was REFUTED (1-2) and should not be cited."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Unmute's TTS-stage latency is reported at ~750ms on a single L40S GPU, improving to ~450ms in Kyutai's own production multi-GPU deployment -- a concrete current figure for the spec's named future migration target, though this covers TTS latency only, not full STT->LLM->TTS round-trip latency.",
    "confidence": "medium",
    "sources": ["https://github.com/kyutai-labs/unmute"],
    "evidence": "3-0 unanimous, verified verbatim against the primary README. Downgraded to medium because it is a single-source figure covering only one pipeline stage; a related claim asserting Unmute achieves sub-second FULL round-trip latency was separately REFUTED (0-3), so full end-to-end latency for this pipeline remains unverified by strong sources."
  }
]</findings>
<caveats>Several optimistic vendor/marketing latency and 'trivial swap' claims did NOT survive adversarial verification and should be treated as unconfirmed: Cerebrium's ~500ms round-trip figure attributed to co-location, Unmute's sub-second full round-trip claim, the claim that cascaded pipelines 'dramatically outperform' native speech-to-speech models, an arxiv source's claim that OpenAI-compatible abstraction makes LLM swapping 'trivial,' and a claim that Pipecat uses a 'SentenceAggregator' for sentence-boundary LLM->TTS streaming (an arxiv-sourced claim that failed 1-2). Treat any of these numbers or mechanisms as unverified if they appear elsewhere. One citation-quality issue was caught and corrected: a claim about Unmute's word-boundary chunking was originally sourced to mintlify.wiki (a third-party auto-generated GitHub mirror, not a primary Kyutai source) -- the corrected citation is Unmute's actual GitHub source files. Time-sensitivity: Pipecat's provider list and Unmute's default model/production model are both fast-moving (the Pipecat/LiteLLM issue thread alone spans March 2025 through 2026 with no resolution), so provider lists and default-model choices should be re-checked at implementation time rather than trusted as static. No surviving claim addresses actual API pricing (Claude Sonnet vs GPT-class vs self-hosted inference cost) or tool-calling reliability specifics for lightweight tasks (reminders/timers/Q&A) -- these were not covered by any claim that passed 3-vote verification.</caveats>
<openQuestions>["What is the actual, currently-measured full STT->LLM->TTS round-trip latency for a Pipecat pipeline using a hosted proprietary LLM (e.g., Claude Sonnet or GPT-4o-class) with faster-whisper + Piper, as opposed to the refuted Cerebrium/Unmute latency claims?", "How reliable is tool-calling (reminders, timers, simple Q&A) specifically through Pipecat's LLM service abstraction when swapping between a proprietary API and a self-hosted open-weight model -- does tool-call format/reliability degrade with smaller open models (e.g., Llama 3.1 8B) versus Claude/GPT-class models?", "What are current (mid-2026) per-minute or per-token cost comparisons across the pipeline options (Claude Sonnet vs GPT-class vs self-hosted vLLM/Ollama inference cost, faster-whisper vs Deepgram STT pricing, Piper vs Kokoro vs ElevenLabs TTS pricing) for a small nonprofit's expected usage volume?", "Are there newer (post-spec-date 2026) frameworks or techniques specifically optimized for accessible, low-latency, voice-only conversational AI for blind users (e.g., specialized turn-taking tuned for screen-reader-adjacent use, or accessibility-specific benchmarks) that neither Pipecat's nor Unmute's general-purpose docs surface?"]</openQuestions>
</StructuredOutput>
